////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Acid Test: Auto Constants
//
// The big-operator heads (sum, sumOver, defint, integral, product, Union,
// Intersect) are auto-declared constants: their meaning is fixed by Lurch
// itself (a validation tool interprets them), so a Rule that uses one
// without a Declare must not treat it as a metavariable.  The list is
// derived from the notation tables (parsers/notation-tables.js
// autoDeclaredConstants) and folded into the systemConstants machinery in
// interpret.js.
//
// The motivating hazard (see the 2026-07-27 roadmap decision log entry):
// sum and defint both parse to 4-ary applications whose first argument is
// a binding, so they are each other's best match targets.  With sum a
// metavariable, the summation split rule in the first section instantiates
// sum := defint and silently justifies a FALSE integral identity (the
// integral split is at c, not c+1).  Auto-declaring the head costs nothing
// expressively: the body, index, and limits stay metavariables, which the
// second section checks.
//
// Each section below uses its own disjoint constants so that rules and
// instantiations cannot cross-justify between sections.  The heads sum and
// product are deliberately NOT declared in the first two sections -
// auto-declaration is exactly what is under test.

Declare =, +, ⋅

➤ "an undeclared sum is not a metavariable: it must not instantiate to defint"
{
  Declare g, p, q, r
  Rule: :{ sum(f,k,a,b) = sum(f,k,a,c) + sum(f,k,c+1,b) }
  defint(g,x,p,q) = defint(g,x,p,r) + defint(g,x,r+1,q) ✗
}

➤ "the same rule still fires on sums: body, index, and limits stay metavariables"
{
  Declare h, u, v, w
  Rule: :{ sum(f,k,a,b) = sum(f,k,a,c) + sum(f,k,c+1,b) }
  sum(h,x,u,v) = sum(h,x,u,w) + sum(h,x,w+1,v) ✔︎
}

➤ "re-declaring an auto-constant is harmless"
{
  Declare product, s, m, n, e
  Rule: :{ product(f,k,a,b) = product(f,k,a,c) ⋅ product(f,k,c+1,b) }
  product(s,x,m,n) = product(s,x,m,e) ⋅ product(s,x,e+1,n) ✔︎
}
